{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785183895,"nonce":"uZ8Us5mxw+DXM37+j5QsBBsS/oQ=","title":"ADR-0062 s5: serve Workspace Exports over NFS from the workspace service","message":"Serve each Export over NFS, one export per capability path, from the workspace service. `nfs-ganesha` in userspace is the trodden path. Includes the deployment wiring (Helm chart, `deploy/local-proc`, `deploy/local-helm`) and the privileged service Pod that ADR-0062's ladder prefers.\n\n**ADR-0062 names this the largest single risk in the design**: a real new dependency implementing a protocol with decades of edge cases.\n\nExploit the property general-purpose NFS never has: an Export has **exactly one client and one writer**, and its lower layer is immutable, so aggressive client attribute caching (high `actimeo`, `nocto`) is safe. That is the specific lever against the many-small-files metadata cost that makes EFS bad at `node_modules`.\n\n\n- [ ] A Step-shaped client can mount an Export by capability path, read lazily and write, and the writes land in the upper\n- [ ] Mount options are set from the one-client-one-writer argument, with the reasoning recorded, not copied from a tutorial\n- [ ] Hard mounts, so a stall is bounded by the existing Step deadline and retried rather than corrupting a build\n- [ ] A dead or restarted server surfaces as a named Attempt failure, not a mystery I/O error (ADR-0027: smart never means mysterious)\n- [ ] `just` recipes cover it; no raw docker/kubectl/helm anywhere\n- [ ] Drain-then-roll upgrade path documented — a Step mounts one replica, so a rollout must stop accepting new Exports and wait for in-flight Steps\n\n\n- s4 (there is nothing to export until Exports exist)","files":null}]}